UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 16, Lesson 2: Fractions of shapes and sets', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nShow 2/3 of a shape and 2/3 of 12 counters.\n\nFEEDBACK\nShape: 2 of 3 equal regions shaded. Set: 12÷3=4, take 2 groups → 8.\n\nWATCH FOR\nShading unequal regions that merely look like \"most of\" the shape is not a named fraction.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nComplete eight tasks: four shape-shading and four set fractions (unit and non-unit). Label each and write one sentence comparing a shape example with a set example.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Strategies","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-PF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Complete eight tasks: four shape-shading and four set fractions (unit and non-unit). Label each and write one sentence comparing a shape example with a set example. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Shade 3/4 of a rectangle split into 4 equal parts. For a set: 3/4 of 8 counters is 6 after sharing into 4 groups of 2.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Shading unequal regions that merely look like \"most of\" the shape is not a named fraction. Correction: Shape: 2 of 3 equal regions shaded. Set: 12÷3=4, take 2 groups → 8.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Shading unequal regions that merely look like \"most of\" the shape is not a named fraction. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set.","Shading unequal regions that merely look like \"most of\" the shape is not a named fraction.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the working\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attemp