e sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use shape with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify set and explain why it matters.","I can practise shade independently on a new prompt.","I can notice equal parts needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShade 3/4 of a rectangle split into 4 equal parts. For a set: 3/4 of 8 counters is 6 after sharing into 4 groups of 2.\n\nTRY FIRST\nShow 2/3 of a shape and 2/3 of 12 counters.\n\nFEEDBACK\nShape: 2 of 3 equal regions shaded. Set: 12÷3=4, take 2 groups → 8.","explain":"Fractions of shapes and sets both need equal parts: shade equal regions of a shape, or take equal groups from a set.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Shading unequal regions that merely look like \"most of\" the shape is not a named fraction. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fraction of a shape beside a fraction of a set.","caption":"Equal parts required in both cases.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shape</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">3/4 of equal regions</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Set</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">3/4 of 8 = 6</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Shade 3/4 of a rectangle split into 4 equal parts. For a set: 3/4 of 8 counters is 6 after sharing into 4 groups of 2. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Shade 3/4 of a rectangle split into 4 equal parts. For a set: 3/4 of 8 counters is 6 after sharing into 4 groups of 2. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShade 3/4 of a rectangle split into 4 equal parts. For a set: 3/4 of 8 counters is 6 after sharing into 4 groups of 2. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correc